# "SAT simplify" (and the other simplify phrasings) on C: the C frontend
# (c optimize), not SPIR-V; options from the words
SAT simplify
```c
unsigned f(unsigned x) { return (x * 8) / 4; }
```
make it faster for zen4 with n in [0, 32)
```C
unsigned rot(unsigned x, unsigned n) { return (x >> n) | (x << ((32 - n) & 31)); }
unsigned shr(unsigned x, unsigned n) { return n < 32 ? x >> n : 0; }
```
peephole with smt, budget 20
```h
static inline unsigned char lo4(unsigned x) { return (unsigned char)(x & 15); }
```
# untagged: C by its types
simplify this
```
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t div7(uint32_t x) { return x / 7; }
```
# untagged, either language: GLSL, with a note
simplify this nofile
```
int twice(int x) { return x + x; }
```
# GLSL tagged ```c for its colours stays GLSL
SAT simplify nofile
```c
uint Unorm16To8(uint c) {
    return (c * 255u + 32895u) >> 16u;
}
```
# ```glsl is GLSL
optimize this nofile
```glsl
uint f(uint x) { return (x * 8u) / 4u; }
```
# a bare block, no words: not used (as for SPIR-V and GLSL)
```c
unsigned g(unsigned x) { return x / 2; }
```
```spv
%a = OpUDiv %uint %x %uint_2
OpStore %out %a
```
# "lower" still lowers
lower this C
```c
int avg(int a, int b) { return (a + b) / 2; }
```
